    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "None of the evaluation methodologies reviewed contain a complete, integrated methodology for evaluating the socio-economic impacts of large earthquakes. Most of these approaches are fairly narrow in scope and consider only a portion of the full impacts of large earthquakes. Most of these approaches are based heavily on informed opinion of experienced experts, but only a few state assumptions and relationships between variables explicitly.\r\n\r\nWe believe that three approaches (ATC-13, ATC-25, and the VSP Benefit-Cost Model) provide the best methodologies for the quantitative modeling of the socio-economic impacts of large earthquakes. These methods all use quantitative relationships between earthquake intensity,physical damage and socio-economic impacts which arise from physical damage. None of these approaches are comprehensive, but these approaches can be generalized to produce a systematic, comprehensive methodology for evaluating socio-economic impacts.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Mizukoshi, Kaoru and Miyamura, Masamitsu and Miura, Yoshikatsu and Yamada, Toshiro and Nakahara, Mitsuharu and Ishida, Hiroshi and Lagorio, Henry J and Olson, Robert A and Scott, Stanley and Goettel, Kenneth A. (1993). Methodologies for Evaluating the Socio-Economic Consequences of Large Earthquakes. CUREE-Kajima Research Report CKI-21. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/sv772gj2426",
